234:The native tests use a fake transport. They verify the shape of the request, that schemas are preserved, replay, and that the catalog is stable. They do not verify that a provider accepts the request or that the model selects the right tool. The offline test commands do not authorize any live request. Before any live batch, Kenneth must approve the number of runs and the token estimate. Log `usage` on every response and report the total, including judge requests and continuations.
242:**Let call logs decide consolidation.** If transcripts show that two tools are always called together, consider merging them. If two facets of one tool are never used together, consider splitting them. Make these decisions from eval evidence, not from a design discussion.
244:**Output schemas are not advertised over MCP.** No client was shown to use them, and they were two thirds of the catalog. `tools/list` no longer carries `outputSchema`. `validate_output` still checks every successful MCP result, so a result that breaks its contract still fails with a visible error. We measured the effect on one build, with the recipe near the end of this guide. With five tools, the catalog went from 9,723 bytes to 3,141, which is from about 2,430 tokens to 785. Only the MCP side saves anything. The in-app adapter has always sent `input_schema` as `parameters` and nothing else, so an in-app turn never carried output schemas. Restore the field only for a client that is shown to read it.
